From b811ddb852e3265c5d2aafc6967ba998c4a9d28e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:58:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] lib/streamaggr: place a sample_timestamps round the
 same in every rule, at its first block

A round processed late, or in blocks a boundary falls between, could still go
to different intervals in different rules: the tokens of a round in one minute
and its cost, in a rule of its own as the _1m_by_* rules have it, in another.
On the lab replay with vmagent held to 300m CPU, two separate rules disagreed in
200 of 400 business-group minutes. Three causes:

- Where a late round went depended on how far each rule's flush had got. Rules
  flush at their own pace, and a round processed while one had written the
  last interval and another not yet went two ways.
- A round's later blocks were placed again, each on its own: a round whose
  first block was the tail of its own interval and whose next block came after
  the following boundary was split two minutes apart.
- Each rule read the clock as a push went from one rule to the next, and a
  flush could take a block decided before it for one decided after.

Now a round goes to the interval it was scraped in if that has not ended by the
boundary before it is processed, and otherwise to the interval it is processed
in. That is decided at the first of its blocks any rule of the config processes
and kept, by scrape timestamp, for the rest of the round in every rule: in a
table shared by the rules of one config, capped at 128k rounds. Every rule
places a push by the same reading of the clock. A flush first waits for the
pushes in progress -- pushes never wait -- and never takes an interval for
written before the clock has reached its end. Only a round still being
processed more than an interval after the one it was put in has its later
blocks go further.

Tests: a late round while one rule's flush is held, a round in two blocks across
a boundary in two rules, a push held across a flush, an early flush with
no_align_flush_to_interval; each fails on the code before. A randomized test of
tokens and cost in separate rules, rounds often in two blocks seconds apart and
each rule's flushes held at random, has 0 split intervals where the code before
had 48. Each of the four mechanisms, broken in turn, fails a test. -race passes.

End to end: lab replay at 300m CPU, 70-120 s behind, 0 of 500 business-group
minutes disagreeing between the two rules; locally, 245M tokens through
rejected rounds, a reload and a restart, exact, 0 of 38000 key-minutes split;
the round split across a flush gives every minute exact.
